Here is a complete, ready-to-compile LaTeX document that demonstrates how to do this.

### The Complete LaTeX Code

You can copy and paste this entire code block into your LaTeX editor (like TeXstudio, Overleaf, or VS Code with LaTeX Workshop) and compile it.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{geometry} % For better page margins
\geometry{a4paper, margin=1in}

% =================================================================
% REQUIRED PACKAGES FOR CHESSBOARDS
% =================================================================

% The primary package for drawing chessboards.
\usepackage{chessboard}

% Highly recommended: Provides high-quality chess fonts (skaknew)
% and many other chess-related commands. chessboard works better with it.
\usepackage{xskak}
% =================================================================


\title{Drawing a Chessboard from FEN in \LaTeX}
\author{A LaTeX Guide}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
This document demonstrates how to render a chessboard diagram in \LaTeX\ using a FEN string. We use the \texttt{chessboard} package, which provides the powerful \texttt{\\fenboard} command.

\section{Example 1: The Starting Position}

The FEN for the standard starting position is: \\
\texttt{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1}

The \texttt{\\fenboard} command only needs the first part of the FEN (the piece placement) to draw the board.

\begin{center}
    % This is the core command to draw the board from the FEN string.
    \fenboard{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR}
\end{center}

\section{Example 2: A Mid-Game Position (Sicilian Defense)}

Let's use a FEN from a few moves into a game: \\
\texttt{r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3}

Again, we just use the piece placement part of the string.

\begin{center}
    \fenboard{r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R}
\end{center}

\section{Customization and Options}

The \texttt{chessboard} package is highly customizable. You can change the board size, show coordinates, and flip the board.

\subsection{Changing Board Size and Adding Labels}

We can use \texttt{\\setchessboard} to set options for all subsequent boards. Here, we'll make the board smaller and add algebraic coordinates.

\setchessboard{
    boardsize=6cm,  % Set the width of the board
    label=true,     % Show rank and file labels (a-h, 1-8)
    showmover=false % Hide the small circle indicating whose move it is
}

\begin{center}
    \fenboard{r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R}
\end{center}

\subsection{Flipping the Board (Black's Perspective)}

You can easily show the board from Black's perspective by using the \texttt{inverse=true} option.

\begin{center}
    % You can also pass options directly to the \fenboard command
    \fenboard[inverse=true]{r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R}
\end{center}

% Resetting to default for any future boards
\setchessboard{boardsize=8cm, label=false, showmover=true}

\end{document}
```

### Breakdown of the Code

#### 1. Preamble
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{geometry}
\geometry{a4paper, margin=1in}
```
This sets up a standard article document with sensible margins.

#### 2. Required Packages
```latex
\usepackage{chessboard}
\usepackage{xskak}
```
*   `\usepackage{chessboard}`: This is the essential package. It provides the environment and commands for drawing the board.
*   `\usepackage{xskak}`: While `chessboard` can function alone, it is highly recommended to load `xskak`. It provides the `skaknew` font, which renders beautiful, scalable vector-based chess pieces. Without it, you might get lower-quality bitmap fonts.

#### 3. The Core Command: `\fenboard{}`
```latex
\fenboard{<fen_piece_placement_string>}
```
This is the command that does all the work. You pass it the first field of the FEN string, which describes the positions of the pieces.

**How FEN notation works for piece placement:**
*   It describes the board from rank 8 down to rank 1.
*   `r, n, b, q, k, p` represent black pieces (rook, knight, bishop, queen, king, pawn).
*   `R, N, B, Q, K, P` represent white pieces.
*   A number from `1` to `8` represents that many consecutive empty squares.
*   A `/` separates the ranks.

The `\fenboard` command intelligently parses this string and places the correct pieces on the board.

#### 4. Customization with `\setchessboard{}`
```latex
\setchessboard{
    boardsize=6cm,
    label=true,
    showmover=false
}
```
This command allows you to set options that will apply to all chessboards that follow it in the document.
*   `boardsize=<dimension>`: Controls the width of the board (e.g., `6cm`, `3in`).
*   `label=true`: Adds the file (`a`–`h`) and rank (`1`–`8`) coordinates around the board.
*   `showmover=false`: The FEN string contains information about whose turn it is (`w` or `b`). By default, `chessboard` draws a small circle next to the board to indicate the active player. This option hides that circle.

#### 5. Inline Options
```latex
\fenboard[inverse=true]{...}
```
You can also pass options to a specific board directly in square brackets. The `inverse=true` option flips the board vertically, showing it from Black's point of view (rank 1 at the top, rank 8 at the bottom).

